$DIR/main.p:21:9: p holds a view of o (o.P(), line 19), which was moved into drop (its parameter takes an O by value) at line 20, so p can no longer be used here
    p looks at memory that o owns, without owning it: the view was made by o.P()
    at line 19. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only
    while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 20 o was
    moved into drop (its parameter takes an O by value), so the view may look at
    memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 20, or move o only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:10: q holds a view of c (f(&c), line 26), which was moved into take (its parameter takes a C by value) at line 27, so q can no longer be used here
    q looks at memory that c owns, without owning it: the view was made by f(&c)
    at line 26. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only
    while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 27 c was
    moved into take (its parameter takes a C by value), so the view may look at
    memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use q before line 27, or move c only after the last use of q
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
